{
  "text": "One of the biggest shopping mall empires in the Philippines started by selling shoes. You see, in 1958, a Chinese immigrant named Henry Sy opened a small shoe store in Manila called ShoeMart. As more customers came in, he expanded his business, selling clothes, accessories, and eventually almost everything a family needed. But in the 1980s, he made a risky decision. While the Philippines was struggling with an economic crisis, Henry began building a massive shopping mall in a relatively undeveloped area of Quezon City. In 1985, SM North EDSA opened its doors. Instead of visiting different shops across the city, Filipinos could now shop, eat, watch movies, and spend time with their families inside one air -conditioned building. Even people who had no intention of shopping had a reason to visit. And the more people visited, the more attractive the mall became to other businesses. Henry repeated this formula across the country, opening SM Megamall and eventually the Mall of Asia. His business expanded beyond shopping malls into banking, hotels, and real estate. And that's how a man who started by selling shoes built an empire that changed how millions of Filipinos spend their weekends.",
